# Playground - BONANZA's course 6 (SPEC.md 128.16.8): wave 6's feel on one
# course, the owner's freeze course at gate 1 and later exploration's sandbox.
# Built for the RUBBER ball; every element sits where a ball from the start
# meets it:
#  - the STEP LADDER: four terraces behind one level apron (4 units), faces
#    of 1.5, 1, half and 2 units, each 3 tiles wide with the apron's 6 tiles
#    of run-up in front of it, so a ball approaches at the cap.  Half a unit
#    rolls up; 1 unit is rubber's Hop and glass's; 1.5 units is rubber's Hop
#    and boost (the BOUNCE CHAIN onto its ledge); 2 units is nobody's.  Every
#    rung is a DEAD END: four tiles of void between neighbours (the boost's
#    radius is 3), and the 2-unit top is outside the reachable set with the
#    Hop and the boost (`bbcourse.py --selfcheck` item 7) - it stands 12 and
#    19 tiles from the 1-unit and 1.5-unit tops, beyond a boosted fall's
#    rebound off the apron, and 5 from the half-unit one;
#  - the GAP LADDER: three notches cut into the apron's front edge, 1, 2 and
#    3 tiles across onto the far floor FF at the same level, with the apron as
#    the run-up.  Every ball Hops 1; rubber Hops 2 (and Hops and boosts it from
#    a wide band); nobody crosses 3, the boost included;
#  - the BRAKE STRIP: off the apron's front right, a run-up three lanes wide -
#    ICE, normal and SAND, the side lanes ending at rails - then three tiles of
#    level floor ending at the VOID, not a rail: a pit sunk in a BASIN 12 units
#    down, so its kill is 15 units under the strip and a ball that overruns
#    falls deep with the camera's ground line held (SPEC.md 128.16.7's
#    fallback case; the depth chosen on the model: GLIDE without the fallback
#    loses the footprint before the kill, with it keeps it and never reverses);
#  - the MAT, level, at the apron's left end beside the 1.5-unit ledge, so a
#    Hop lands on it (no boost on a mat);
#  - a PAD for each ball on FF beside the goal;
#  - the GOAL, at the end of a ROLLING route (the apron, FF's right, no Hop);
#  - the DIAGONAL SECTION (SPEC.md 128.24, wave D5): off the apron's right
#    end a LANE straight DOWN the screen, 32 px wide between two straight
#    diagonal RAILS (16 px, 1.5 units over the lane), stepping down as it
#    goes - a RAMP (`ramp s`, one unit), then a half-unit diagonal STEP whose
#    FRONT face is a 4-line ledge - onto a CHAMFERED PLATFORM whose four sides
#    are straight 45-degree course EDGES over the void.  Every piece of it is
#    a half (the rails' sides, the lane's sides, the step, the platform's
#    edges) or a whole cell; no quarter.  A dead end on purpose, off every
#    route: a half unit rolls up and the ramp's unit wants speed, so the way
#    back is RETURN or the edge.
# No ramp anywhere, so no up-ramp is within 6 tiles of the rungs or the gaps
# (SPEC.md 128.16.5.2).  Width 128: the apron and its four rungs need 24
# tiles along +wx, which width 64's footprints cannot hold.
course PLAY "Playground"
width  128
ball   rubber
# the PLAYGROUND shows the CGA's other palette at gate 1 (SPEC.md 128.16.6): 10h
palette 0
# its song, named since SPEC.md 128.23 added a seventh (the title screen's): without one, course 6
# would wrap onto it (course_song)
music  fairway

# the apron, and behind it the step ladder, left to right 1.5, 1, half and 2 units
floor rect  10 14 34 20  flat 4
floor rect  10 11 13 14  flat 5.5
floor rect  17 11 20 14  flat 5
floor rect  24 11 27 14  flat 4.5
floor rect  31 11 34 14  flat 6
# the mat, level with the apron, at its left end
floor rect   7 15 10 18  flat 4
surface bounce rect 7 15 10 18
# FF, the gap ladder's far floor, joined to the apron on its right; the three
# notches, 1, 2 and 3 tiles; FF's rails and the goal.  The front rail has a GAP at wx 17 (2026-11-17): the
# WIND YARD's gust gate, below
floor rect  10 20 23 29  flat 4
hole  rect  10 20 19 21
hole  rect  13 21 19 22
hole  rect  16 22 19 23
wall  rect  10 29 17 30  flat 4.5
wall  rect  18 29 19 30  flat 4.5
wall  rect  23 23 24 29  flat 4.5
goal  rect  19 29 23 31  flat 4
# the brake strip's run-up, ice | normal | sand, the side lanes' rails, the
# strip itself, and the pit in the basin
floor rect  23 20 33 23  flat 4
surface ice  rect 24 20 27 23
surface sand rect 30 20 33 23
floor rect  24 24 34 34  flat -8
wall  rect  24 23 27 24  flat 4.5
wall  rect  30 23 33 24  flat 4.5
floor rect  27 23 30 26  flat 4
hole  rect  27 26 30 33
# a pad for each ball
pad rubber 22 23
pad steel  22 25
pad glass  22 27

start 21 16
# The ROLLING route (the tour's and the route proofs'): FF's right side to the goal
way 21 16
way 20 22
way 20 30

# Par: the AUTHORED demo's time rounded up (SPEC.md 128.16.8, as MOON's and PEAK's).
# wave 7 (SPEC.md 128.18.3): `par` became `limit`; the PLAYGROUND's is explicit, its
# AUTHOR demo tours every rung (27 s), past the tool's 15 s from the autopilot's route
limit 40
# The design time (CD3); the course budget reads it (SPEC.md 128.15.8).
target 30

# the FINISH clock (wave 8R, SPEC.md 128.22.6: every finish has a clock in the CGA window centred on it,
# which the finish sequence flashes); placed off the demo's and the autopilot's routes - their replays
# are the same with it and without it, so the build re-stamps the demo's identity
clock 18 28

# the DIAGONAL SECTION (SPEC.md 128.24, wave D5): `box a0 a1 s0 s1` is a rectangle ON THE SCREEN
# (16 a0 <= gx - gy <= 16 a1, 16 s0 <= gx + gy <= 16 s1); every number odd, so no cell is quartered.
# The lane, a = 15 .. 17 (32 px), from inside the apron's right end down to the platform: level, a unit's
# ramp, level, the half-unit step at s 67 (its front face), and the platform's own height
floor box 15 17 49 57 flat 4
floor box 15 17 57 59 ramp s 57 59 4 3
floor box 15 17 59 67 flat 3
floor box 15 17 67 69 flat 2.5
# the platform: 224 x 96 px, its corners cut two cells deep along cell edges
floor box 9 23 69 81 chamfer 2 flat 2.5
# ...and it FLOATS (SPEC.md 128.27.2): a dead end out over the void, its straight edges hanging 3 units
# (today's faces) where every other face over the void reaches the course's ground, the basin's floor less
# 3 units - the tour's one `float` (the pathway floats whole), so the statement is exercised and previewed
float box 9 23 69 81 chamfer 2
# the rails, 16 px each, 1.5 units over the lane beside them; the funnel s 49 .. 55 is open both sides
wall  box 13 15 55 57 flat 5.5
wall  box 13 15 57 59 ramp s 57 59 5.5 4.5
wall  box 13 15 59 69 flat 4.5
wall  box 17 19 55 57 flat 5.5
wall  box 17 19 57 59 ramp s 57 59 5.5 4.5
wall  box 17 19 59 69 flat 4.5

# wave 9 (SPEC.md 128.25.15): the PROBE family, which proves the family hooks and the per-play state
# with NO physics - its element changes no cell physics reads and no rule, so the demo replays as
# without it.  On the apron where the demo rolls (grounded), so every hook fires in its replay; its
# flag-like lane flies once the ball has entered its cell, and a fall furls it again (RESPAWN)
uses probe glass pickups wind water spike magnet archway boost gate
prop probe 16 16 reset respawn

# wave 9 (SPEC.md 128.25.8, 128.25.18): the GLASS SECTION, off FF's left edge, off every route: a FAR
# PLATFORM (a steel pad on it) joined to FF by two one-tile paths over the void - the CRACK PATH (four
# cracked tiles, wy 24), which glass rolls across at leisure and steel and rubber only at speed, and the
# GLASS PATH (four glass tiles, wy 27), invisible until rolled onto, its first tile's jagged edge at FF
floor rect  3 23  6 29  flat 4
floor rect  6 24 10 25  flat 4
floor rect  6 27 10 28  flat 4
prop crack 9 24
prop crack 8 24
prop crack 7 24
prop crack 6 24
prop glass 9 27
prop glass 8 27
prop glass 7 27
prop glass 6 27
pad steel 4 25
# SPEC.md 128.32.8: the GROUP LAB, on the far platform - three `hole wide` cracks edge to edge along its back
# row (wy 23: their 4-block lanes share blocks, so their art is GROUP lanes posed by the three together, and
# each breaks on its own), and a TURN of three glass tiles (4, 27) - (5, 27) - (4, 26), glass look 11 in its
# 2 x 3 lanes, whose corner pair (4, 26) - (5, 27) shares a block row and so groups (refused before 128.32)
prop crack 3 23 hole wide
prop crack 4 23 hole wide
prop crack 5 23 hole wide
prop glass 4 27
prop glass 5 27
prop glass 4 26

# wave 9 (SPEC.md 128.25.9, 128.25.19, moved here at integration: glass's section holds FF's left edge): the
# PICKUPS YARD, left of the mat at the apron's level - an
# hourglass, a speed and a jump - joined to the apron's left end by a row above the mat and a row below
# it (wy 14 and 18; wy 19 - 22 stay void, so the yard is no way round the gap ladder's 1-tile notch and
# no step towards the glass section's far platform), and behind it the RIDGE, 2 units up, the step
# ladder's "nobody's" height: over a Hop (5.7 px and the 4 px climb) and over rubber's boost (9.3 px);
# the jump's own Hop (13.8 px) climbs it, and a jump with rubber's boost STACKED on its contact (18.0
# px) clears it with room.  Nothing is up there but the view, and it is no trap: its front edge is open,
# so a ball rolls off it back into the yard.  Every timed pickup is short (3 s each), so their reach
# regions stay off the 2-unit rung (wx 31).  The ridge is 3 tiles wide with FOUR of void between it
# and the 1.5-unit rung (wx 6 - 9), so no ball reaches it from that ledge
floor rect   3 13  7 19  flat 4
floor rect   7 14 10 15  flat 4
floor rect   7 18 10 19  flat 4
floor rect   3  8  6 13  flat 6
prop hourglass 3 18
prop speed     5 14 for 3
prop jump      6 17 for 3

# SPEC.md 128.25.18.1 (the owner 2026-11-17: a bigger hole; floating cracks that vanish): the
# CRACK LAB, off the apron's right edge in the start's rows - roll +x (right and down the screen) from the
# start.  A rubber pad and a steel pad on the apron by the lab (30, 18), (31, 18), off the run-ups.  Two rows out to an end platform and back:
#  - wy 19, grounded, a run-up and past the end platform two `band` cracks edge to edge (the 2 x 2 lane's
#    tallest hole), a dead end (the `wide` hole is the GROUP LAB's, on the glass section's far platform)
#  - wy 16, a FLOATING bridge (`float`): three cracks among plain floating tiles that VANISH - the tile
#    and its faces gone, the void showing - one cell apart (a vanishing crack's lanes are 4 blocks across)
floor rect  34 19 45 20  flat 4
floor rect  34 16 41 17  flat 4
floor rect  41 16 43 20  flat 4
float rect  34 16 41 17
prop crack 43 19 hole band
prop crack 44 19 hole band
prop crack 35 16
prop crack 37 16
prop crack 39 16
# rounds 2 - 4 (SPEC.md 128.27.2.1, the owner's; THE recommended cracked tile, 128.25.18.1): a THIN BRIDGE at wy 14, off the apron's back row - `float
# ... thick`: half a unit thick (wx 34 - 37), then one unit (38 - 40), stepping down at (40, 15) onto the
# 3-unit bridge at wy 16.  Its FIVE cracks 35 - 39, edge to edge, are a narrow walkway drawn in their 2 x 3
# lanes, each vanishing on its own; it starts and ends on FULL tiles (34, 14) and (40, 14) (plain `thick` cells,
# drawn whole), the walkway's end corners drawn with them and left as their edges
floor rect  34 14 41 15  flat 4
floor rect  40 15 41 16  flat 4
float rect  34 14 38 15  thick 0.5
float rect  38 14 41 16  thick 1
prop crack 35 14
prop crack 36 14
prop crack 37 14
prop crack 38 14
prop crack 39 14
pad rubber 30 18
pad steel  31 18

# SPEC.md 128.30.11: the SHAPED-FLOOR SECTION of wave C4 (ff8eabb29), put back behind the step ladder, and the
# radius-3 PIT that did not fit beside it (128.28.8), on a plate at the apron's height joined to the walkway's left
# end.  As one part this course is 65,513 bytes under D and 76,507 under C: it is a TWO-PART course (128.30.4.1)
floor rect  16  9 29 10  flat 4
floor rect  21 10 22 14  flat 4
chute 2 round depth 10 way 18 9 4 way 18 5 way 24 5 6 way 24 9 4
chute 1 round depth 5 way 26 8 4 way 26 4 way 28 4 way 28 8
floor rect   8  3 17 10  flat 4
pit   12 6 4 radius 3 depth 16

# 2026-11-17, the props round: the WIND YARD (SPEC.md 128.34) and the GUARD RAIL (128.35), in FRONT of FF, its
# floor at FF's height and FLOATING (faces 3 units deep, not to the basin's ground: the picture's bytes).  Out
# through the gap in FF's front rail (wx 17), the ball(s) each gate is for taken on FF's pads beside the gap:
#  - the GUST GATE: the stub out of the gap, one wide, a GUSTING fan in the gap blowing +x off the stub's open
#    side, 64 steps in every 256 (its WISP over the stub) - every ball crosses between gusts; glass, blown twice
#    as hard, has the shortest window;
#  - the HUB (its pads moved to the prop alley's arch, 2026-11-18: FF's steel and glass pads are two cells away);
#  - the THIN PATH east (wy 36), a STEADY fan blowing +y off its front edge, strength 10 (two static squiggle
#    clusters): rubber and glass go over whatever they do, steel leans into it and crosses to the END PLATE,
#    its HOURGLASS steel's prize; a see-through GUARD RAIL on the plate's far and front edges stops a steel
#    ball that arrives at speed without hiding it (MEASURED on the model: steel across at every speed tried,
#    rubber and glass over the edge);
#  - the PIT WIND west: a `lift` updraft over the hub's west column and a one-column pit, flagged (its
#    streamer), and the LEDGE 2.5 units up beyond it: glass rolled onto it is launched with no Hop, held -x
#    lands on the ledge by its HOURGLASS (glass's prize, apex 62 - 72 px); rubber and steel are not lifted and
#    roll into the pit.  A fall off the hub, the thin path or into the pit respawns on the hub; off the stub,
#    at the start.
floor rect  17 29 18 33  flat 4
floor rect  15 33 22 38  flat 4
floor rect  22 36 26 37  flat 4
floor rect  26 35 29 38  flat 4
floor rect  12 34 14 37  flat 6.5
float rect  17 30 18 33
float rect  15 33 22 38
float rect  22 36 26 37
float rect  26 35 29 38
float rect  12 34 14 37
# (the end plate's FRONT guard, `guard rect 26 38 29 39`, is left off: with the clocks occluding
# (SPEC.md 128.18.5.4) the course reached 257 occluder masks of the 256 a record's mask byte names;
# the far guard below still stops a fast steel ball)
guard rect  29 36 30 38
prop blower 17 29  face +x  region 17 30 17 32  strength 8  gust 64 192
prop blower 22 36  face +y  region 22 36 25 36  strength 10
prop blower 15 35  face up  region 14 34 15 36  strength 16  top 5  lift  flag
prop hourglass 27 36
prop hourglass 12 35

# PROP ALLEY (2026-11-18, the props round's merged families on the owner's stress test; SPEC.md 128.33 - 128.40):
# in FRONT of the wind yard's hub, 2 units up, floating.  Down the RAMP off the hub's front (21, 38 - 41, half a
# unit a tile - every ball climbs back up it, a -y BOOST PAD at its foot pushing a slow steel ball on); the SPINE
# (18 - 23, 42 - 43) west and east of its foot.  Its pieces, each for the ball(s) named:
#  - the WATER column in the hub's front row (18, 37): rubber and glass ride over it, steel sinks to the bed and
#    leaves by the FRONT ARCH onto the SPIKES at its mouth (18, 39 - 40), which steel rides and rubber pops on -
#    steel's shortcut down, the owner's pairing of the two steel-only mechanisms;
#  - a POP-UP SPIKE row on the spine's 1-wide neck (22, 42): every ball going east times it;
#  - the HOVER LANE (wy 42): an ARCHWAY over a STEEL PAD (24, 42) - under its 2-unit arch no ball can Hop or
#    boost over the pad, so every ball leaves it as steel - then a 2-cell gap under two HANGING MAGNETS on posts
#    behind it: steel hovers across (MEASURED on the model: rubber, glass and steel all reach the far plate);
#  - the MEMBRANE LANE (wy 44): the same gap bridged by the crystal MEMBRANE, glass's alone (rubber and steel
#    fall through);
#  - the FAR PLATE (28 - 31, 42 - 44), which the basin's exit (below) also reaches.
floor rect  21 38 22 42  ramp y 38 42 4 2
floor rect  18 42 24 44  flat 2
floor rect  18 38 19 42  flat 2
floor rect  24 42 26 43  flat 2
floor rect  23 44 28 45  flat 2
floor rect  26 41 28 42  flat 4
floor rect  28 42 32 45  flat 2
float rect  18 42 24 44
float rect  18 38 19 42
float rect  24 42 26 43
float rect  23 44 28 45
float rect  26 41 28 42
float rect  28 42 32 45
water tile  18 37  top 4 bed 2
arch  18 37 +y
prop spikes 18 39 19 41
prop popspikes 22 42 period 128 on 64 seed 5
hole  tile  22 43
archway tile 24 42 along x
pad steel 24 42
prop magnet 26 41 face down +y
prop magnet 27 41 face down +y
prop membrane 26 44 28 45
prop boost 21 42 face -y strength 40

# THE BASIN'S EXIT (the owner, 2026-11-18: "a lift to that bottom area that you can't get out of"): a ball off
# the brake strip's side lands in the BASIN 8 units down (rubber bounces, steel is stunned, glass shatters and
# respawns) and could leave it only by the hole.  The course's LIFT (one a course, SPEC.md 128.12.4.6) stands in
# its front-right corner (33, 33): its slab docks at the basin's floor and at the HIGH RIM (32, 33), 8 units
# up; nothing stands in the shaft's screen column in front of it (the slab is drawn behind what does).  From
# the rim, south: a PLATE (32, 34) that opens the GATE on the 1-wide neck (32, 35) (SPEC.md 128.41), a timed
# DRAWBRIDGE (31 - 32, 38), half-unit steps up to the far plate.  MEASURED on the model: rubber and steel ride
# up and walk the whole exit when they board with the slab low; a fall respawns in the basin.  A HUMP (SPEC.md
# 128.44) stands on the hub, a dome over its middle.
floor tile  32 33  flat 0
hole  tile  33 33
floor rect  31 34 33 41  flat 0
hole  tile  31 35
floor tile  32 41  flat 0.5
floor tile  32 42  flat 1
floor tile  32 43  flat 1.5
float tile  32 33
float rect  31 34 33 41
float rect  32 41 33 44
float rect  31 38 33 39  thick 0.5
prop platform 33 33 8 dwell 21
prop plate 32 34 channel ring
prop gate 32 35 channel ring along y
prop bridge 31 38 33 39 period 256 on 128 seed 17
hump 17 35 4 height 5
